hadiths[41]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1288","It is narrated from Sayyida Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: When Hafsa bint Abdur Rahman entered her third menstrual period, she changed her residence. Ibn Shihab (may Allah have mercy on him) said: I mentioned this matter to Amrah bint Abdur Rahman, and she said: ""Urwah has spoken the truth. In fact, people objected to Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), saying: Allah Almighty says: 'Divorced women shall keep themselves in waiting for three periods (__QUR_N__),' so Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) said: You have spoken the truth. Do you know what is meant by 'qurūʼ'? 'Qurūʼ' means purity."
"1289","Abu Bakr bin Abdur Rahman narrates: I found all the jurists saying the same thing that Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) said, (that ""qur’"" means purity)."
"1290","It is narrated from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: ""When a divorced woman begins to experience the blood of her third menstruation, she has completed her waiting period ('iddah) from her husband."
"1291","Sulaiman bin Yasar (may Allah have mercy on him) narrates that Ahwas's wife entered her third menstrual period, and Ahwas passed away in Syria. He had divorced his wife. Muawiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote a letter to Zaid bin Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) and asked him about the issue. Zaid (may Allah be pleased with him) replied in writing: ""When she entered the blood of the third menstruation, she and her husband were free from each other; they can no longer inherit from one another."
"1292","Zaid bin Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) stated that when a divorced woman begins her third menstrual period, she completes her waiting period (iddah)."
"1293","It is narrated from Nafi‘ that Ibn ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) said: ""When a man divorces his wife and she enters into her third menstrual period, then this woman becomes separated from her husband and he from her. Neither will she inherit from him, nor will he inherit from her."
"1294","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Utbah that Umar bin Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""A slave may marry two women, and he will give only two divorces, and the waiting period (‘iddah) of a slave woman will be two menstrual cycles; if she does not menstruate, then the waiting period will be two or one and a half months."
"1295","It is narrated from a man of Thaqif that he heard Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: ""If I had the power, I would make her waiting period one and a half menstruations."" Upon this, a man said: ""You may make her waiting period one and a half months,"" so Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) remained silent."
"1296","It is narrated from Nafi‘ that Ibn ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) said: ""An umm walad whose master passes away will observe ‘iddah for one menstrual cycle."
"1297","Ibn Musayyib (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated that Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""If a woman is divorced and then she experiences one or two menstrual cycles, and after that her menstruation stops, she should wait for nine months. If pregnancy becomes apparent, then that is the duration of her waiting period. Otherwise, after nine months, she should wait for another three months, and then her waiting period will be complete."
"1298","It is narrated from Ibn Musayyib and Sulayman bin Yasar that Talihah was married to Rashid Thaqafi. Her husband gave her an irrevocable divorce, and then during her waiting period (‘iddah), she married another man. So ‘Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) flogged her and her second husband and separated them. Then ‘Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: “Any woman who marries during her ‘iddah, if her (new) husband has not consummated the marriage with her, then they will be separated, and she will complete the remaining ‘iddah from her first husband, and this (second husband) will be counted as one of those who sent a marriage proposal. If they have consummated the marriage, then they will also be separated, and this woman will observe the waiting period (‘iddah) for both her first and second husband, and she can never marry the (second) husband again.” Sa‘id said: Due to consummation, the woman will also be entitled to the dowry."
"1299","It is narrated from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) that he gave the verdict regarding a woman who married during her waiting period (iddah) that the two should be separated (i.e., the marriage should be annulled), and due to having relations with the woman, she is entitled to her dowry (mahr). The waiting period (iddah) which the woman disrupted, she must complete, and she must also observe the waiting period for the second husband."
"1300","It is narrated from Abdullah bin Utbah that Sabi’ah bint Harith (may Allah be pleased with her) gave birth to a child a few days after the death of her husband. Abu Sanabil bin Ba’kak (may Allah be pleased with him) passed by her and said, “You have adorned yourself for marriage, while your waiting period (‘iddah) is four months and ten days.” Sabi’ah mentioned this to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), so he (ﷺ) said: “Abu Sanabil has spoken incorrectly, or as Abu Sanabil said, the matter is not so. You have completed your ‘iddah, you may marry.”"
"1301","Abu Salamah bin Abdur Rahman narrated that Ibn Abbas and Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with them) were asked about a pregnant woman whose husband passes away. Ibn Abbas said, ""She must observe the longer of the two waiting periods."" And Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""When she gives birth, her waiting period is over."" Then Abu Salamah went to Umm al-Mu'mineen Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) and asked her about this issue. She said that Subai'ah al-Aslamiyyah (may Allah be pleased with her) gave birth half a month after her husband's death. Two men sent her marriage proposals—one was young and the other was middle-aged. She sent a proposal to the young man. The middle-aged man said, ""Your waiting period is not over yet,"" and her family was away. That man hoped that when her family returned, they would give him preference over the young man. When she came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), he said: ""Your waiting period is over; you may marry whomever you wish."
"1302","It is narrated from Sulaiman bin Yasar (may Allah have mercy on him) that Ibn Abbas and Abu Salamah (may Allah be pleased with them) differed regarding a woman whose husband died and she gave birth to a child a few days later. Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""She must observe the longer of the two waiting periods."" But Abu Salamah (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""When she experiences postnatal bleeding, her waiting period is over."" Sulaiman said that Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) also arrived and said, ""I am with my nephew, i.e., Abu Salamah (meaning my ruling is the same)."" Finally, they sent Ibn Abbas’s freed slave, Kuraib, to Umm Salamah, and he asked her about this issue. Then he returned to them and reported that she said: Subai’ah (may Allah be pleased with her) gave birth to a child a few days after her husband’s death, and she mentioned this to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), so he said to her: ""Your waiting period is over, so you may marry."
"1303","It is narrated from Miswar bin Makhramah (may Allah be pleased with him) that Sabi’ah Al-Aslamiyyah (may Allah be pleased with her) gave birth to a child a few days after the death of her husband. Then she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and sought permission to marry, so he (peace and blessings be upon him) granted her permission."
"1304","It is narrated from Nafi‘ that Ibn ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) was asked about a woman whose husband has died and she is pregnant. Ibn ‘Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) said: When she gives birth, her ‘iddah will end. An Ansari man informed him that ‘Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: If a woman gives birth while her husband is still on the bed, not yet buried, even then her ‘iddah has ended."
"1305","Abdur Rahman bin Abi Bakr narrated that Hibban bin Munqidh Al-Ansari divorced his wife while he was healthy, and that woman was breastfeeding their daughter. Seventeen months passed and she did not menstruate; due to breastfeeding, she was not getting her period. Then, seventeen or eighteen months after the divorce, Hibban became ill. I said to him: ""Your wife wants to become your heir."" He said to his family: ""Take me to Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him)."" They took him to him, and he informed him about his wife's intention. Ali bin Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) and Zaid bin Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) were also present with Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him). Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) said to them: ""What do you both think?"" They both said: ""In our opinion, if he dies, the woman will inherit from him, and if she dies, he will inherit from her, because she is neither among those women whose menstruation has stopped, nor among those girls who have not yet reached the age of menstruation. Therefore, she is in her waiting period for menstruation."" (Upon hearing this) Hibban returned home and took his daughter from her. When the breastfeeding stopped, she had one period, then a second period, and before she could have a third period, Hibban passed away. So she observed the waiting period for a woman whose husband has died and also inherited from his wealth."
"1306","It is narrated from Muhammad bin Yahya bin Haban that his grandfather had a Hashemite and an Ansari woman in his marriage. He divorced the Ansari woman while she was breastfeeding, and a year passed in which he died and the woman did not menstruate. The woman said: ""I will inherit from him because I have not menstruated."" They brought their dispute to Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him), and he ruled in favor of inheritance for the Ansari woman. Upon this, the Hashemite woman spoke ill of Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him), so he said: ""This is the work of your cousin,"" referring to Ali bin Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"1307","It is narrated from Abdu Khair that Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said regarding the man who married and died before consummation, and he had not fixed the dowry for his wife: the woman is entitled to a share of inheritance and must observe the waiting period (‘iddah), but she is not entitled to the dowry."
"1308","It is narrated from Nafi' (may Allah have mercy on him) that he relates regarding the daughter of 'Ubaydullah bin 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and her mother, the daughter of Zayd bin Khattab, that ('Ubaydullah bin 'Umar's daughter) was married to the son of 'Abdullah bin 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), and he passed away before consummation, and no dowry had been fixed for her. When her mother demanded the payment of the dowry for her, Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""There is no dowry for her, and if she were entitled to a dowry, we would not withhold it nor would we wrong this woman."" Her mother refused to accept this, so they appointed Zayd bin Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) as the judge, and he ruled that there is no dowry for her, but she is entitled to inheritance."
"1309","It is narrated from Humaid bin Nafi’ that Zainab bint Abi Salama related three hadiths to him. She said that Zainab (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated: I came to Umm al-Mu’minin Umm Habibah (may Allah be pleased with her) at the time when her father Abu Sufyan had passed away. Umm Habibah (may Allah be pleased with her) asked for perfume in which there was a yellowish tinge of khaluq or something else mixed in it. Then a slave girl applied it to her, then she herself applied it to her cheeks, and then said: “By Allah! I had no desire for perfume, but I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: ‘It is not permissible for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to mourn for anyone for more than three days, except for her husband (for whom the mourning period is) four months and ten days.’”"
"1310","And Zainab narrated that after that I came to Zainab bint Jahsh (may Allah be pleased with her) when her brother had passed away, so she called for perfume and applied it, then said: ""By Allah! I had no desire for perfume, but I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say from the pulpit that it is not permissible for any woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to mourn for any deceased person for more than three days, except for her husband, for whom the mourning period is four months and ten days."
"1311","And Zainab narrated that I heard from my mother, Umm Salama (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: A woman came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, “O Messenger of Allah ﷺ! My daughter’s husband has passed away, and her eyes are in bad condition. Can she apply kohl?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied, “No,” and said it two or three times, each time saying, “No.” Then he said, “The (prescribed waiting period) is four months and ten days. In the time of ignorance, you used to throw dung after a year.” (Meaning, in the time of ignorance, the waiting period was a year, and now you cannot be patient for four months and ten days.) Zainab (may Allah be pleased with her) said: When a woman’s husband died (in the time of ignorance), she would enter a small room and wear the worst clothes, and would not use perfume or anything else until a year passed in that state. Then, to come out of the waiting period, an animal—a donkey, goat, or some bird—would be brought, and she would hold it. It was rare that she would hold a part of the animal and it would not die. Then she would come out of the waiting period, and she would be given dung, which she would throw away. Then she would use perfume or something else. Imam Shafi’i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: “Hifsh” means a small house made of hair or bricks, and “al-qabs” means to hold a part of the animal with your fingers, and “qabz” means to hold with the whole palm."
"1312","Narrated by Aisha and Hafsa (may Allah be pleased with them): The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""It is not permissible for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to mourn for any deceased person for more than three days, except for her husband, for whom the mourning period is four months and ten days."
"1313","It is narrated from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said regarding a woman whose husband is missing: ""She will not marry (someone else)."
"1314","It is narrated from Yasar bin Abi Al-Hakam that Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said regarding a woman whose husband is missing: ""When her husband returns and his wife has married someone else, then if he wishes, he may divorce her, and if he wishes, he may keep her in marriage, and the woman will not be given this choice."
"1315","It is narrated from Fatimah bint Qais (may Allah be pleased with her) that Abu Amr bin Hafs gave her an irrevocable divorce (the third and final divorce) while he was in Syria. Then a long hadith is mentioned, and in it she said that she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and mentioned this to him. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""You are not entitled to maintenance from him."" And he ordered her to spend her waiting period ('iddah) in the house of Umm Sharik. Then he said: ""She is a woman whom my companions frequently visit, so spend your 'iddah in the house of Ibn Umm Maktum. He is a blind man, so you may remove your clothes (i.e., relax your hijab) there."
"1316","Maymun bin Mehran says: I came to Madinah and asked about the greatest scholar of Madinah, so I was sent to Sa'id bin al-Musayyib (may Allah be pleased with him). I asked him about a woman who has been given an irrevocable divorce, and he replied: She will observe her 'iddah in her husband's house. So I said, ""What about the hadith of Fatimah bint Qais?"" He expressed anger and said: The incident of Fatimah has ruined people, although there was harshness in her speech, which was unpleasant for her husband's relatives, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) ordered her to observe her 'iddah in the house of Umm Maktum."
"1317","It is narrated from Qasim and Sulaiman bin Yasar, both state that Yahya bin Saeed bin As sent an irrevocable divorce to the daughter of Abdur Rahman bin Hakam, so Abdur Rahman bin Hakam brought her from her husband's house. When Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) learned of this, she sent a message to the governor of Madinah, Marwan bin Hakam, and said, ""O Marwan, fear Allah and return the woman to her (husband's) house."" In the narration of Sulaiman, it is mentioned that Marwan said: ""Abdur Rahman prevailed over me (i.e., he did not accept my word)."" And in the narration of Qasim, Marwan replied: ""Do you not know about the case of Fatimah bint Qais?"" Upon this, Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""Even if you had not mentioned the incident of Fatimah, you would still have no right (because that is not evidence for you)."" In response, Marwan said: ""If Fatimah's moving from her husband's house was due to tension among the husband's relatives, then that situation is also present here."" (i.e., there is also tension among them)."
"1318","It is narrated from Muhammad bin Ibrahim that Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) used to say, ""O Fatimah! Fear Allah. Do you know why this command was given? (That is, to spend the waiting period in the house of Ibn Umm Maktum instead of the husband's house.)"
"1319","It is narrated from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding the statement of Allah Almighty, ""except in case they commit a clear indecency"" (__QUR_N__: At-Talaq: 1), he said: If her indecency becomes evident to her family, then it is permissible to send her out of the house."
"1320","It is narrated from Fatimah bint Qais (may Allah be pleased with her) that Abu Amr bin Hafs gave her the third and final divorce while he was in Syria and not present there (i.e., he sent the divorce from there). His agent sent her some barley, at which she became upset. So he said: ""By Allah! You have no right over us at all."" (Hearing this) she went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and mentioned this matter to him, so he ﷺ said: ""It is correct, your maintenance (expenses) are not his responsibility."
"1321","It is narrated from Abu al-Zubair that Jabir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The divorced woman is entitled to maintenance as long as she is not forbidden (i.e., as long as the irrevocable divorce has not occurred), and when she becomes forbidden (i.e., when three divorces have taken place), then she should be provided for in a good manner."
"1322","Ibn Jurayj (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated that Ata (may Allah have mercy on him) said: For a thrice-divorced pregnant woman, the husband is responsible for nothing except to spend on her because of her pregnancy, and if she is not pregnant, then her maintenance is not upon the husband."
"1323","Fari’ah bint Malik bin Sinan (may Allah be pleased with her) narrated that she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: “Send me to my parents’ house among Banu Khudrah, because her husband had gone out in search of his runaway slaves. When he reached the place of Qudum, the slaves killed him.” Fari’ah sought permission from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) to go to her family home because her husband had not left any house of which he was the owner. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “Yes (you may go).” When I returned to the room or the mosque, he (peace and blessings be upon him) called me or ordered that I be called, so I was summoned. He (peace and blessings be upon him) asked: “What did you say?” She says: Then I again narrated the incident of my husband, so he (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “Stay in your (husband’s) house until your waiting period (‘iddah) is complete.” She says: I observed the ‘iddah there for four months and ten days. She further says: When ‘Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) was the caliph, he sent someone to me and inquired about this issue, so I informed him, and he judged accordingly."
"1324","Hisham bin Urwah (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated from his father that he said regarding the rural woman whose husband has passed away: ""She will stay where her family resides."
"1325","In another chain, Hisham narrated from his father and Ubaydullah bin Abdullah bin Utbah, similar to the previous hadith or with the same meaning."
"1326","Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) said: There is no maintenance for a widow; her share of inheritance is sufficient for her."
"1327","It is narrated from Salim bin Abdullah (may Allah have mercy on him) that Ubaidullah (may Allah have mercy on him) used to say: ""It is not permissible for any woman to spend even a single night outside her home during the waiting period ('iddah) after her husband's death or divorce."
"1328","It is narrated from Nafi' (may Allah have mercy on him) that the daughter of Sa'id bin Zaid (may Allah be pleased with him) was married to Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him), and Abdullah gave her an irrevocable divorce, so she left his house, which Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) disliked."
